.Sh DESCRIPTION
A torrent manages a single download or seed operation.
It owns peer connections, piece state, storage access, and the piece picker.
.Pp
.Fn mbt_torrent_new
creates a torrent with explicit configuration;
.Fa cfg
specifies the event loop, infohash, peer ID, storage backend, picker, and timers.
.Fn mbt_torrent_free
destroys it and all associated resources.
.Ss Life cycle
.Fn mbt_torrent_set_metadata
imports the info dict (from a
.Pa .torrent
file or completed BEP 9 fetch) so the torrent can begin downloading.
Before metadata arrives the torrent stays in the
.Dq init
state.
.Pp
.Fn mbt_torrent_add_peer
attaches an outbound connection; the torrent takes ownership of
.Fa conn .
.Fn mbt_torrent_add_peer_incoming
attaches an inbound connection (already completed handshake).
.Ss Queries
.Fn mbt_torrent_state
returns one of:
.Bl -tag -width MBT_TORRENT_SEEDING
.It Va MBT_TORRENT_INIT
No metadata.
.It Va MBT_TORRENT_DOWNLOADING
Actively receiving pieces.
.It Va MBT_TORRENT_STALLED
Incomplete, no progress possible.
.It Va MBT_TORRENT_SEEDING
All pieces present and verified.
.El
.Pp
.Fn mbt_torrent_progress
returns a fraction between 0 and 1 of the download completed.
.Fn mbt_torrent_complete
returns true if the torrent has all pieces.
.Fn mbt_torrent_bytes_downloaded
returns the total number of bytes received from peers since the torrent was added.
.Pp
.Fn mbt_torrent_piece_map
fills an output array with per-piece state:
.Dq 0
(pending),
.Dq 1
(active/requested),
.Dq 2
(have).
Suitable for TUI piece-map rendering.
.Ss File list
Multi-file torrents expose their file layout via
.Fn mbt_torrent_file_count
and
.Fn mbt_torrent_file .
Each entry has a relative path, byte offset, and length.
.Ss Rate limiting
.Fn mbt_torrent_set_upload_rate
and
.Fn mbt_torrent_set_download_rate
cap per-torrent bandwidth; 0 means unlimited.
These override the session-level limits set via
.Xr mbt_session 3 .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
Functions returning
.Ft int
return 0 on success or a negative errno value on failure.
Functions returning pointers return NULL on error.
